# How can we draw what someone wants?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is an indifference curve? Why do they bend toward the origin?"
next: {"title":"Where is the best choice?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/75-where-is-the-best-choice.cast"}

## Torn between bundles
use: indifference_budget
with:
    labels: false
    axis_numbers: true
    income: 20
    price_x: 1
    price_y: 1
    preference: balanced
    curves: 3
    show_optimum: false
    x_good: Shoes
    y_good: Hats
    x_max: 24
    y_max: 24
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 110
      w: 700
      h: 560
sources:
    - id: thurstone1931
      title: The Indifference Function
      authors: L. L. Thurstone
      year: 1931
      finding: 'Journal of Social Psychology 2: 139–167. Estimated a subject''s indifference curves from many hypothetical choices between bundles of hats, shoes and coats (e.g. 8 hats and 8 shoes vs 6 hats and 9 shoes); reported one subject in detail.'
    - id: hicksallen1934
      title: A Reconsideration of the Theory of Value
      authors: J. R. Hicks and R. G. D. Allen
      year: 1934
      finding: 'Economica 1(1–2): rebuilt the theory of consumer choice on the marginal rate of substitution and an ordinal ranking of bundles, with no measurable utility.'

    portrait thurstone x 860 y 470 width 190 of "Louis Leon Thurstone" cameo true hidden true
    dot pA #3d3833 at.data [8,8] cites ["thurstone1931"] hidden true
    dot pB #3d3833 at.data [9,6] hidden true
    text lA "8 hats + 8 shoes" at.data [8,9.7] font_size 24 hidden true
    text lB "6 hats + 9 shoes" at.data [13.6,5.2] font_size 24 hidden true
    dot t1 #2f6b8f at.data [6,6] hidden true
    dot t2 #2f6b8f at.data [9,4] hidden true
    dot t3 #2f6b8f at.data [3,12] hidden true
    dot t4 #2f6b8f at.data [12,3] hidden true
    text cue_ic "Indifference curve" #2f6b8f at.data [5.5,15.5] font_size 24 hidden true
    dot p10 #3d3833 at.data [10,10] hidden true
    label l10 "10 + 10" right attach_to p10 hidden true
    arrow better #b5482e from.data [4.2,4.2] to.data [14.5,14.5] hidden true
    label lbetter "Better" left #b5482e attach_to better hidden true
    text r1 "1st" #8f887c at.data [24.8,1.6] font_size 22 hidden true
    text r2 "2nd" #8f887c at.data [24.8,4.3] font_size 22 hidden true
    text r3 "3rd" #8f887c at.data [24.8,10.3] font_size 22 hidden true
    text order "ranks, not amounts" #8f887c cites ["hicksallen1934"] x 860 y 560 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "How can we draw what someone wants?"

    hide budget_line

How can we draw what someone wants? You can't photograph a want. But in 1931 the psycho (@draw thurstone@)logist Louis Thurstone tried.
    draw axes

B: He asked his subject question after question, like this one: eig (@draw pA lA@)ht hats and eight shoes, or six hat (@draw pB lB@)s and nine shoes?

    quiz "Which would you take?" judge false
        right: Noted. There is no wrong answer to a taste.
        * 8 hats + 8 shoes
        * 6 hats + 9 shoes
        * Can't decide

No answer is wrong. Thurstone didn't care which bundle won. He was hunting for the third answer: pairs his subject couldn't choose between.
    highlight pA pB lA lB

    erase thurstone

B: Torn, you mean. Why would torn be useful?
    fade pA pB lA lB to 0.2

Because torn means equally good. Say, with our own numbers, someone (@draw t1@) is torn between six hats with six pairs of shoes, and four (@draw t2@) hats with nine pairs.

And just as torn between  (@draw t3@)twelve hats with three pairs, and thr (@draw t4@)ee hats with twelve.

    erase pA pB lA lB

B: So join t (@draw ic_1@)he dots. Every bundle on this curve is equally good to her: an i (@draw cue_ic@)ndifference curve.

Now give her ten (@draw p10 l10@) hats and ten pairs. More of both (@highlight ic_1@) beats everything on that curve.

    erase cue_ic

B: And ten and ten has its own torn partners,  (@draw ic_2@)so it gets a curve of its own, further out.

    erase l10

So a want (@draw ic_3@) becomes a map: curves of equal liking, and the further from  (@draw better lbetter@)the corner, the better.

B: But the curves carry no amounts of pleasure. Only (@draw r1@) an order (@draw r2@): first, s (@draw r3@)econd, third.

That was the step John Hicks and Roy Allen took in 193 (@draw order@)4: they built the theory of choice on the order alone.

    pause 0.5

## Why the curve bends
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 22
    'y':
      - 0
      - 20
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 110
      w: 900
      h: 560
vars:
    s:
      value: 2
      min: 2
      max: 12
      step: 0.5
    cost:
      expr: 36/s - 36/(s+1)

    axes ax x_label Shoes y_label Hats cross corner ticks true hidden true
    curve ic #2f6b8f expr 36/x x_from 1.85 x_to 21 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    dot pA #3d3833 at.on ic at.x 2 hidden true
    arrow run1 #8f887c from.x 2 from.y 18 to.x 3 to.y 18 hidden true
    arrow drop1 #b5482e from.x 3 from.y 18 to.x 3 to.y 12.4 hidden true
    label l_drop1 "−6 hats" right #b5482e attach_to drop1 hidden true
    dot pC #3d3833 at.on ic at.x 12 hidden true
    arrow run2 #8f887c from.x 12 from.y 3 to.x 13 to.y 3 hidden true
    text l_drop2 "−¼ hat" #b5482e at.data [13.6,5] font_size 26 hidden true
    text mrs "MRS: hats per extra pair" #b5482e at.data [11,14] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot P #b5482e at.on ic at.x 2 bind {"at.x":"s"} hidden true
    text readout "One more pair: {cost:2} hats" #b5482e at.data [11,15] font_size 30 hidden true
    curve chord dashed thick #8f887c expr "20 - x" x_from 2 x_to 18 hidden true
    dot pE #3d3833 at.on ic at.x 18 hidden true
    dot mid #8a5fa8 at.data [10,10] hidden true
    label l_mid "mix: 10 + 10" above-right #8a5fa8 attach_to mid hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch m top-right work ["MRS = hats ÷ shoes","18 ÷ 2 = 9","3 ÷ 12 = ¼"] font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Why the curve bends"

B: Every curve on that map sags toward the cor (@draw ic@)ner. Why not a straight line? Let's walk along hers.
    draw ax

B: Start where she has eighteen ha (@draw pA@)ts and only two pairs of shoes.

How many hats would she g (@draw run1@)ive up for a third pair?

B: On her curve,  (@draw drop1 l_drop1@)six. Eighteen hats down to twelve, and she's no worse off.

Now jump far down the curve: t (@draw pC@)welve pairs, only three hats.

    quiz "Now she has twelve pairs and three hats. Is one more pair worth more hats to her than before, or fewer?" judge false voice b
        right: Watch how flat the curve is down here.
        * More
        * The same
        * Fewer

B: Fewer, and far fewer. A  (@draw run2@)thirteenth pair is worth about a  (@draw l_drop2@)quarter of a hat to her.

Of course. Wi (@highlight pA effect circle@)th a pile of hats, one more hat means little and shoes mean a lot. Wit (@highlight pC effect circle@)h a pile of shoes, it's the other way round.

B: That swap rate has a name: the marginal ra (@draw mrs@)te of substitution. How much of one thing you'd give for one more of the other.

    fade run1 drop1 l_drop1 run2 l_drop2 to 0.2

    erase mrs

    draw P readout

And  (@animate s 12 duration 4@)it falls as you slide down the curve. The falling rate is the bend.

    erase P readout run1 drop1 l_drop1 run2 l_drop2

B: Another way to see it: draw a straight lin (@draw chord pE@)e between two lopsided bundles. Its middle, ten a (@draw mid l_mid@)nd ten, lies beyond her curve.

So she'd rather have a mix than a pile of either. That's all the bend says.
    highlight mid effect pulse

    erase chord mid l_mid pE

    animate s 2 duration 0.4

    draw P readout

B: Try it: slide her along the curve and watch what one more pair costs in hats.
    explore params ["s"]

    erase P readout

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: There is a one-line formula behind that slide.

@math

    erase mathq

The steepness of the curve  (@draw m_box m_line_1@)at any point is her hats divided by her shoes, for this curve.

B: At two pairs and eightee (@draw m_line_2@)n hats, that's nine hats for a tiny step more of shoes.

At twelve pairs and (@draw m_line_3@) three hats, a quarter. The ratio falls as you slide, and that's the bend.

    erase m

@after

    erase mathq

    pause 0.3

## Not every want bends
    path pc_ax thick #3d3833 points [[55,500],[55,190],[325,190]] hidden true
    text pc_t "Corner" #b5482e x 190 y 575 font_size 34 hidden true
    text pc_xl "Left shoes" #8f887c x 190 y 162 font_size 22 hidden true
    text pc_yl "Right shoes" #8f887c x 110 y 525 font_size 22 hidden true
    path pc_c0 #b5482e points [[125,485],[125,260],[315,260]] smooth false style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path pc_c1 #b5482e points [[205,485],[205,340],[315,340]] smooth false style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    group pc members ["pc_ax","pc_xl","pc_yl","pc_c0","pc_c1"] hidden true
    path ps_ax thick #3d3833 points [[375,500],[375,190],[645,190]] hidden true
    text ps_t "Straight" #8a5fa8 x 510 y 575 font_size 34 hidden true
    text ps_xl "Brand A eggs" #8f887c x 510 y 162 font_size 22 hidden true
    text ps_yl "Brand B eggs" #8f887c x 430 y 525 font_size 22 hidden true
    path ps_c0 #8a5fa8 points [[375,310],[495,190]] smooth false style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path ps_c1 #8a5fa8 points [[375,380],[565,190]] smooth false style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path ps_c2 #8a5fa8 points [[375,450],[635,190]] smooth false style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    group ps members ["ps_ax","ps_xl","ps_yl","ps_c0","ps_c1","ps_c2"] hidden true
    path pb_ax thick #3d3833 points [[695,500],[695,190],[965,190]] hidden true
    text pb_t "Bent" #2f6b8f x 830 y 575 font_size 34 hidden true
    text pb_xl "Shoes" #8f887c x 830 y 162 font_size 22 hidden true
    text pb_yl "Hats" #8f887c x 750 y 525 font_size 22 hidden true
    path pb_c0 smooth #2f6b8f points [[709,475.7],[710.7,445.1],[712.6,417.8],[714.7,393.4],[717,371.6],[719.7,352.1],[722.6,334.8],[725.9,319.2],[729.7,305.4],[733.8,293],[738.5,282],[743.7,272.1],[749.5,263.3],[756.1,255.5],[763.4,248.5],[771.6,242.2],[780.8,236.6],[791.1,231.6],[802.7,227.2],[815.6,223.2],[830,219.6],[846.3,216.4],[864.4,213.6],[884.7,211.1],[907.5,208.8],[933,206.8]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path pb_c1 smooth #2f6b8f points [[733.8,473.3],[738.5,443],[743.7,415.9],[749.5,391.7],[756.1,370.1],[763.4,350.8],[771.6,333.5],[780.8,318.2],[791.1,304.4],[802.7,292.2],[815.6,281.2],[830,271.5],[846.3,262.7],[864.4,254.9],[884.7,248],[907.5,241.8],[933,236.2]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    group pb members ["pb_ax","pb_xl","pb_yl","pb_c0","pb_c1"] hidden true

    card title "Not every want bends"

So a bent curve (@draw pb pb_t@) means a mix beats a pile. Does every taste bend like that?

B: Take left shoes and  (@draw pc_ax pc_xl pc_yl@)right shoes. A third left shoe, with only two right ones, is worth nothing to you.

So her curves h (@draw pc_c0 pc_c1 pc_t@)ave corners. The goods only count in pairs: economists call them perfect complements.

B: Now the opposite: two b (@draw ps_ax ps_xl ps_yl@)rands of eggs she can't tell apart.

She swaps o (@draw ps_c0 ps_c1 ps_c2 ps_t@)ne for one, anywhere. The swap rate never falls, so the line never bends: perfect substitutes.

B: And hats and shoes sit in between. Bent, but no corner.
    highlight pb

    hide pc ps pb pc_t ps_t pb_t

    cards sortq check each
        Corner: Skis & ski boots
        Straight: Two petrol brands
        Bent: Coffee & tea, Fish & bread

Your turn: put each pair of goods under the shape you'd expect for most people.
    ask "Which shape would most people's curves take for each pair? Sort the cards." on sortq store srt stage own
        right: All {srt.total} sorted.
        wrong: You got {srt} of {srt.total}: here, skis corner, petrol straight, the rest bend.

    erase sortq

    show pc ps pb pc_t ps_t pb_t

B: So the shape is the taste. It says how easily one thing can stand in for another.
    highlight pc ps pb

    pause 0.4

## Can you really ask?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Thurstone would have loved a subject like you.
    good: You read the map well. Mind the swap rates.
    poor: 'Look at the shape first: it tells you the taste.'
    none: The curves will still be here when you come back.
use: indifference_budget
with:
    labels: false
    axis_numbers: true
    income: 20
    price_x: 1
    price_y: 1
    preference: balanced
    curves: 2
    show_optimum: false
    x_good: Shoes
    y_good: Hats
    x_max: 24
    y_max: 24
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 110
      w: 700
      h: 560
sources:
    - id: wf1942
      title: The Empirical Derivation of Indifference Functions (in Studies in Mathematical Economics and Econometrics in Memory of Henry Schultz)
      authors: W. Allen Wallis and Milton Friedman
      year: 1942
      finding: 'Criticised Thurstone''s 1931 experiment: "For a satisfactory experiment it is essential that the subject give actual reactions to actual stimuli"; conjectural responses to hypothetical stimuli do not satisfy this (pp. 179-180, quoted in Moscati, ''Measuring utility'', SIE paper).'
    - id: mt1969
      title: The Experimental Determination of Indifference Curves
      authors: Kenneth R. MacCrimmon and Masanao Toda
      year: 1969
      finding: 'Elicited indifference curves from real choices, including cash against French pastries that subjects had to eat in the laboratory (Review of Economic Studies 36(4): 433–451).'

    scratch src cites ["wf1942"] work ["“…actual reactions","to actual stimuli.”","Wallis & Friedman, 1942"] x 810 y 540 font_size 26 hidden true
    portrait friedman x 850 y 270 width 150 of "Milton Friedman" cameo true hidden true
    scratch mt cites ["mt1969"] work ["1969: cash or pastries","eaten on the spot"] x 820 y 540 font_size 26 hidden true
    text conf "Mostly agreed" #5f8a4e x 855 y 520 font_size 40 hidden true
    text conf2 "one person's map: hard" #8f887c x 855 y 465 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Can you really ask?"

    hide budget_line

B: Hold on. Thurstone's sub (@draw ic_1 ic_2@)ject never got a single hat. He only said what he'd pick.
    draw axes

That was exactly the complaint. In 1942 Allen (@draw src_box src_line_1 src_line_2 src_line_3@) Wallis and Milton Friedman wrote that a satisfactory experim (@draw friedman@)ent needs actual reactions to actual stimuli.

B: Answers about made-up bundles, they said, don't satisfy that.
    highlight src_line_1 src_line_2

    erase src friedman

So later experimenters made the choices real. In 1969, Kenneth MacCrimmon an (@draw mt_box mt_line_1@)d Masanao Toda offered cash and French pastries, and the pastries ha (@draw mt_line_2@)d to be eaten in the lab.

    erase mt

B: So how sure are we about the bend?
    highlight ic_1 ic_2

For ordinary goo (@draw conf@)ds, mostly agreed: most people spread their money over many things instead of piling up one. Measurin (@draw conf2@)g one person's whole map is still hard.

    pause 0.2

    quiz "Sara is torn between 2 teas + 8 biscuits and 8 teas + 2 biscuits. Her curves bend the usual way. Now she's offered 6 teas + 6 biscuits. What does she do?" voice b
        intro: Three questions on new cases.
        right: Takes 6 + 6. A bent curve puts the halfway mix, 5 + 5, at least level with her two bundles, and 6 + 6 is more of both.
        wrong: A mix beats a pile, and more beats less: here, she takes 6 + 6.
        + Takes 6 + 6
        * Is torn again
        * Can't tell

    quiz "Ola has twenty coffees and one croissant a week; Kari has five of each. Same tastes. Who would give up more coffees for one more croissant?"
        right: Ola. With a pile of coffee, one more cup means little, so he'd give many for a croissant.
        wrong: Where on the curve is someone with a pile of coffee? Here: Ola.
        + Ola
        * Kari
        * The same

    quiz "For most people, which pair has straight indifference curves?" voice b
        right: The $10 bill and two fives. They swap at a fixed rate anywhere, so the line never bends.
        wrong: Straight means a fixed swap rate: here, the $10 bill.
        + A $10 bill and two $5 bills
        * Left gloves and right gloves
        * Bread and cheese

    erase conf2

So yes, a want can be drawn. Not as (@highlight ic_1 ic_2@) an amount of pleasure, but as a map of the swaps someone would accept. Lay a price tag across that map, and you can find the best choice.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
